/*!
\page qtbridges-csharp-models.html
\title Using C# Models with QML Views
\brief How to select which C# types to expose to QML Views.

\section1 Filtering code generation sources using C# attributes

It's possible to adjust which types and type members are included and excluded
from code generation by using the attributes described below.

\section2 Ignore types

The simplest way of filtering out a type, regardless of where it is defined -
either in the current module or in an external assembly - is by listing that
type in a \c{[Qt.IgnoreType]} assembly-level attribute.

\code
[assembly: Qt.IgnoreType(typeof(List<int>), typeof(List<string>))]

// This class will be included
public class Foo
{
    // These 2 methods will be excluded due to ignored parameter types
    public void Bar(List<int> arg) { }
    public void Bar(List<string> arg) { }

    // This method will be included
    public void Bar(List<double> arg) { }
}
\endcode

\section3 Generic types

For generic types, for example, \c {List<T>} where \c{T} is a type variable,
the \c{typeof()} facility is not available. To ignore a generic type and all of
its specializations, the string form of the generic type must instead be used.

\code
[assembly: Qt.IgnoreType("System.Collections.Generic.List`1")]

// This class will be included
public class Foo
{
    // These 3 methods will be excluded due to ignored parameter types
    public void Bar(List<int> arg) { }
    public void Bar(List<string> arg) { }
    public void Bar(List<double> arg) { }
}
\endcode

\section2 Ignore type definition

It's possible to exclude a type defined in the current project's code by adding
a \c{[Qt.Ignore]} attribute to the type declaration.

\code
// This class will be excluded
[Qt.Ignore]
public class Foo
{ }
\endcode

\section3 Ignore type member definition

Similarly, a member of a type defined in code can also be excluded from code
generation using a \c{[Qt.Ignore]} attribute.

\code
// This class will be included
public class Foo
{
    // This property will be excluded
    [Qt.Ignore]
    public int Bar { get; set; }
}
\endcode

\section2 Ignore type hierarchy

The \c{[Qt.IgnoreType]} attribute can also be used to filter out entire type
hierarchies, that is any type that derives from the root type listed in the
attribute. This is achieved by specifying \c{Inherited = true} in the attribute
instantiation.

\code
[assembly: Qt.IgnoreType("System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary`2", Inherited = true)]

// This class will be excluded as it derives from an ignored type hierarchy
public class Foo : Dictionary<int, string>
{
    ...
}
\endcode

\section3 Include sub-type of ignored type hierarchy

It's still possible to include types deriving from an ignored type hierarchy.
These must be opted-in to the code generation by using the \c{[Qt.Include]}
attribute.

\code
[assembly: Qt.IgnoreType(typeof(Foo), Inherited = true)]

// This class will be ignored (ignored base type)
public class Foo
{ }

// This class will be ignored (derived type of ignored base type)
public class Oof : Foo
{ }

// This class will be included (opted-in by attribute)
[Qt.Include]
public class Bar : Foo
{ }
\endcode

\section1 Accessing C# types from QML Views

Once the desired C# types have been selected for code generation, they can be
accessed from QML Views.

For example, given the following C# type:

\code
using Qt.Bridge.Utils;

namespace MyProject
{
public class MyClass
    {
        public int MyProperty { get; set; }
    }
}
\endcode

To access C# types from QML, instantiate them in QML and access their members
via a \c {.} operator. The member names match the C# names, but are started in
lowercase:

\code
import QtQuick
// ...
Rectangle {
    width: 400; height: 400

    MyClass {
        id: myClassInstance
    }

    Text {
        text: "MyProperty value is " + myClassInstance.myProperty
        anchors.centerIn: parent
    }
}
\endcode

*/
